Apologies if I misinterpreted you.  You said that the point system
"should be left alone", and the game was far too complex for a number
to capture the skill with which it was played, "or the progress which
was achieved".  Then you listed a whole bunch of factors that have
nothing to do with the progress which was achieved.

I was pointing out that most of your list wasn't necessary to
measuring progress, and proposing that any new system should be
directly based on the necessary achievements in the game to reach its
stated end-goal.  I think such a system would be a good one,
especially in terms of helping new players who have not yet ascended
keep a record of how far they're getting.

Certainly you're not going to come up with a system that measures the
elegance or skill with which a game was played, but that doesn't mean
that the current system can't be improved or that progress through the
game is completely impossible to measure.  You can't necessarily
measure it on an absolute step-by-step scale (was my opening move left
better or worse than going right or quaffing that !healing I started
with?), but you can identify major milestones along the way to winning
and use them to let people know whether they're getting closer to the
end-goal in broad terms.  And that would be a lot more useful than a
system that really rewards kind of arbitrary events.



